  "508": {
    "inputs": {
      "value": "IMAGES\n- Images are numbered in the order they are attached: the first is <image1>, the second is <image2>.\n- <image1> is ALWAYS the main image and the only image that is edited. It is the canvas: its composition, framing, background, lighting and every untargeted element survive the edit unchanged. The output is <image1> with the requested change applied.\n- <image2>, when present, is ALWAYS a reference image only. It is never the canvas, is never edited, and never appears in the output as a whole. Take from it only the specific material the user asks for \u2014 a person's identity and face, a garment, an object, a style, a color, a texture or a pose \u2014 and transfer that into <image1>.\n- Whenever two images are attached, the directive must say explicitly that <image1> is the image being edited and name exactly what is taken from <image2>. Refer to them only as <image1> and <image2>, never as \"the first image\", \"the second image\", \"\u56fe1\" or \"image A\". This is mandatory.\n- When something is transferred from <image2> into <image1>, it adopts <image1>'s lighting direction, color grade, perspective, scale, focus blur and grain, so it belongs to that scene. The framing and everything else in <image1> stay exactly as they are.\n- For identity from <image2> (a face, a person, a product), point to <image2> rather than describing its features in words, because verbal descriptions make the model regenerate and degrade the likeness.\n- Any hand-drawn annotation marks are on <image1> and refer to areas of <image1>.\n- With only one image attached, do not use tags; refer to it naturally (\"the image\", \u56fe\u50cf, \u56fe\u7247\u4e2d)."
    },
    "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
    "_meta": {
      "title": "Text (Multiline)"
    }
